  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1258 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nHeat is here to stay as we begin the second week of August, thanks \nto the synoptic pattern remaining largely dominated by high \npressure. Probabilistic data even suggests 10-40% chances for highs \nto locally surpass 105 degrees any afternoon Monday through \nWednesday. Breezy surface winds of 15-25 mph, along with low \nrelative humidity values will lead to recurring elevated fire \nweather conditions on a daily basis across most of the forecast \narea as well.\n\nDeterministic and ensemble data show the ridge gradually becoming \nsuppressed as the jet stream atop the US - Canadian border sags \nslightly south through the week. Thankfully, the resulting southerly \nreturn flow should draw and retain higher moisture content over the \nregion daily, especially later in the work week. POPs will be rather \nlimited and confined to northern portions of the Panhandles Mon - \nWed, but are slated to increase in coverage and likelihood by Thu - \nFri. Both the ECMWF and GFS are currently forecasting PWAT values \nabove the 90th percentile of climatology by Friday, which should be \ntaken with a grain of salt one week out, but is still promising \nnonetheless. Even if rain chances wane, there is higher confidence \nthat lower temperatures in the 80s and 90s will take over behind a \nfront to end next week.\n\nHarrel",
